  "command": "cat > /app/paper.txt << 'PAPER_END'\n# Research Advances in Code Agents and Red-teaming\n\n## Abstract\n\nThe rapid advancement of Large Language Models (LLMs) has given rise to a new class of AI systems known as code agents—autonomous agents capable of generating, executing, and debugging code. These agents have revolutionized software development workflows by enabling dynamic code execution, interactive programming, and automated problem-solving. However, their growing capabilities also introduce significant security risks that must be carefully studied and mitigated. This paper presents a comprehensive survey of recent advances in code agent technology and the emerging field of red-teaming techniques designed to evaluate their safety and robustness. We examine the architecture and capabilities of modern code agents, analyze the security vulnerabilities they expose, and review state-of-the-art red-teaming methodologies. In particular, we highlight recent work on automated red-teaming frameworks that can systematically probe code agents for vulnerabilities across diverse attack surfaces. Our analysis reveals that while code agents demonstrate remarkable potential, they remain susceptible to prompt injection, arbitrary code execution, and unsafe tool use. We discuss the importance of proactive security testing through red-teaming and propose directions for future research in building more secure and trustworthy code agents.\n\n## 1. Introduction\n\nCode agents represent one of the most significant developments in the intersection of artificial intelligence and software engineering. By combining the reasoning capabilities of Large Language Models with tools for code execution, file manipulation, and environment interaction, code agents can perform complex programming tasks autonomously—from writing simple scripts to developing entire software projects.\n\nThe capabilities of code agents have evolved rapidly. Modern implementations, such as those based on advanced LLM architectures, can:\n- Generate code from natural language descriptions\n- Debug existing code by analyzing error messages\n- Run code in sandboxed environments and iteratively refine solutions\n- Interact with external APIs and services\n- Read and write files, manage repositories, and deploy applications\n\nDespite these impressive capabilities, the autonomous nature of code agents introduces novel security challenges. Unlike traditional software where inputs and outputs are well-defined, code agents can execute arbitrary commands, access sensitive resources, and make decisions that may have far-reaching consequences. This combination of high autonomy and broad capabilities makes security a critical concern.\n\nRed-teaming—the practice of systematically probing systems for vulnerabilities through adversarial testing—has emerged as a crucial methodology for ensuring the safety of AI systems. In the context of code agents, red-teaming involves crafting adversarial prompts, test cases, and attack scenarios designed to expose vulnerabilities in how agents handle potentially malicious inputs or unexpected situations.\n\nThis paper surveys recent advances in code agent technology and red-teaming methodologies, with a particular focus on automated approaches to security testing. We structure our discussion as follows: Section 2 reviews related work on code agents and red-teaming. Section 3 discusses the security threats facing code agents. Section 4 examines red-teaming methodologies and tools. Section 5 presents challenges and future directions. Finally, Section 6 concludes with a summary and outlook.\n\n## 2. Related Work\n\nThe field of code agents has seen significant research activity in recent years. Early work focused on demonstrating the feasibility of LLM-based code generation tools such as GitHub Copilot and Codex. Subsequent research extended these systems to include execution capabilities, creating agents that could not only generate code but also run it, debug errors, and iteratively improve their solutions.\n\nRecent work has begun to systematically study the security properties of code agents. One notable contribution is RedCodeAgent: Automatic Red-teaming Agent against Diverse Code Agents [1] (arXiv:2510.02609), which presents an automated framework for red-teaming code agents. This work introduces a systematic approach to generating adversarial prompts and attack scenarios that target various vulnerabilities in code agents, including prompt injection attacks, unsafe code execution, and improper permission handling. The authors demonstrate that their framework can identify diverse attack vectors across different types of code agents, highlighting the importance of comprehensive security testing.\n\nThe broader field of red-teaming for AI systems has also seen rapid growth. Early red-teaming efforts focused primarily on text-based LLMs, investigating issues such as toxic output generation, bias, and information leakage. More recent work has extended red-teaming to multimodal systems, agentic systems, and systems with tool-use capabilities. These studies have revealed that AI agents are vulnerable to a wide range of attacks that would not be possible in non-agentic systems, including attacks that exploit the agent's ability to execute code, access APIs, and make autonomous decisions.\n\nResearch on adversarial examples for LLMs has also informed code agent security. Studies have demonstrated that carefully crafted prompts can bypass safety measures, manipulate agent behavior, and induce the generation of malicious code. These findings underscore the need for robust defense mechanisms and systematic testing procedures.\n\nFinally, the concept of \"agent-as-a-pentester\" has emerged, where AI agents themselves are used to perform security testing. This approach, explored in works such as RedCodeAgent [1], leverages the reasoning capabilities of LLMs to generate diverse and creative attack scenarios, potentially outperforming human red-teaming teams in breadth and automation.\n\n## 3. Security Threats in Code Agents\n\nCode agents face a unique threat landscape that combines vulnerabilities from both LLMs and traditional software systems. Key threat categories include:\n\n### 3.1 Prompt Injection\n\nPrompt injection attacks attempt to manipulate the agent's behavior by crafting inputs that override safety instructions or intended behavior. In code agents, successful prompt injection can lead to execution of arbitrary code, access to unauthorized resources, or generation of malicious code.\n\n### 3.2 Unsafe Code Execution\n\nCode agents typically execute generated code in sandboxed environments. However, these sandboxes can potentially be escaped, allowing agents to access host resources, modify system configurations, or execute commands on behalf of the attacker.\n\n### 3.3 Malicious Tool Use\n\nCode agents that interact with external APIs, databases, or services may inadvertently expose sensitive data or perform unauthorized actions when presented with adversarial inputs. Tool misuse can also occur when agents are tricked into making calls to malicious endpoints.\n\n### 3.4 Information Leakage\n\nCode agents may inadvertently expose sensitive information contained in their context, such as API keys, configuration details, or proprietary code, through their responses or actions.\n\n## 4. Red-teaming Methodologies\n\nRed-teaming code agents requires methodologies that can systematically explore the space of possible inputs and agent behaviors. Recent work has proposed several approaches:\n\n### 4.1 Automated Prompt Generation\n\nAutomated frameworks use LLMs to generate adversarial prompts that target specific vulnerabilities. These frameworks can explore diverse attack strategies and can evolve their attack strategies based on previous results.\n\n### 4.2 Diverse Attack Surface Coverage\n\nEffective red-teaming of code agents must cover all components of the agent architecture, including:\n- The language model's reasoning and code generation capabilities\n- The code execution environment\n- Tool APIs and integrations\n- Feedback loops for iterative improvement\n\n### 4.3 Evaluation Metrics\n\nRed-teaming efforts should employ quantitative metrics to measure:\n- Vulnerability discovery rate\n- Attack success rate across different agent types\n- Severity of discovered vulnerabilities\n- Diversity of attack strategies\n\nThe work by Guo et al. [1] demonstrates the effectiveness of automated red-teaming, showing that their framework can identify a wide range of vulnerabilities across diverse code agent implementations, validating the importance of systematic security testing.\n\n## 5. Challenges and Future Directions\n\nSeveral challenges remain in the field of code agent security:\n\n1. **Scalability**: As code agents become more capable, testing their security properties becomes increasingly complex. Scalable testing frameworks are needed.\n\n2. **Adaptive Attacks**: Attackers will likely develop strategies that adapt to defenses, requiring red-teaming frameworks that can evolve their attack strategies.\n\n3. **Standardization**: Standardized benchmarks and evaluation protocols for code agent security are needed to enable fair comparison between different agents and defense mechanisms.\n\n4. **Human-in-the-loop Testing**: While automated red-teaming is powerful, human expertise remains crucial for understanding the implications of discovered vulnerabilities and for identifying novel attack vectors.\n\n5. **Defense Mechanisms**: Beyond testing, research is needed on effective defense mechanisms that can protect code agents against a wide range of attacks without compromising their utility.\n\n## 6. Conclusion\n\nCode agents represent a powerful new paradigm in software development, enabling unprecedented levels of automation and productivity. However, their autonomous nature and broad capabilities introduce significant security risks that must be addressed. Red-teaming has emerged as a critical methodology for evaluating and improving the security of code agents. Automated red-teaming frameworks, such as those explored in recent work like RedCodeAgent [1], offer promising approaches to systematically identify vulnerabilities across diverse code agent implementations.\n\nAs code agents continue to advance in capability and adoption, the security community must prioritize research into robust defense mechanisms, standardized evaluation protocols, and effective testing methodologies. Only through proactive and systematic security testing can we ensure that code agents develop and deploy safely, protecting users and organizations from potential harm.\n\n## References\n\n[1] Chengquan Guo, Chulin Xie, Yu Yang et al., \"RedCodeAgent: Automatic Red-teaming Agent against Diverse Code Agents,\" arXiv:2510.02609, 2025.\nPAPER_END"
}'}

